From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emmanuel Velez Garcia <velezgarciaemmanuel25@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 22:39:25 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Budget the repaint of mass block changes

Reported symptom: a mine reset took heap usage from around 900 MB to
2400 MB, while the same reset on another 1.8 fork stayed flat and filled
the mine in visibly rather than all at once. A schematic paste behaved
the same way.

That is not a leak, it is a burst of garbage, and the mechanism is the
dirty chunk flush. Past 64 changed blocks in a chunk, PlayerChunk stops
recording individual positions and instead accumulates a section bitmask,
and the flush then sends PacketPlayOutMapChunk for every section that
changed. A reset touching the full height of a chunk is therefore a
near-complete chunk packet - and because that packet is not
groundUpContinuous, it misses PaperSpigot's ChunkMap cache entirely and
allocates a fresh array every time. flush() drained the entire dirty
queue every tick, so every chunk a reset touched was rebuilt, serialised
and compressed inside one tick, once per watching player. A hundred
chunks with ten players nearby is on the order of 170 MB of short-lived
garbage in a single tick.

chunks.dirtyFlush.maxBytesPerTick, default 2 MB, spreads that over the
following ticks. The cost is priced before the packet is built, from the
chunk's section count and the number of players who will receive it, so
a chunk that gets held back costs nothing to skip. Only the section
resend is priced: a single block change is a few bytes and a multi-block
change is bounded by 63 of them, so both are waved through.

Nothing is lost, only delayed - a chunk that waits keeps its dirty state
and is sent from whatever it looks like when its turn comes, which is if
anything more correct. One chunk always goes through per tick, so a
single very large chunk cannot wedge the queue, and 0 restores the old
all-at-once behaviour.

/vs perf chunks reports how many ticks a world spread repaints over, so
the budget can be seen working rather than assumed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

